---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import dadabase from '../assets/portfolio/dad-a-base.jpg';
import bakery from '../assets/portfolio/bakery-shell.jpg';
import solitaire from '../assets/portfolio/solitaire.jpg';
import PortfolioCard from '../components/PortfolioCard.astro';
---

<Layout>
	<main>
		<h1>My Projects</h1>
		<PortfolioCard
			title="The Dad-a-base"
			href="https://jokes.tylerbaker.dev"
			src={dadabase}
			alt="A 90s-inspired website with a red brick background. A header with a wooden background texture shows a joke that says 'When I am stresed I like to iron clothes. It is a great way to let off steam.'"
			tech="Hono + JSX, SVG graphics, Sqlite"
			ps="P.S., I am hosting this one on my homelab! It's running from my Raspbery Pi!"
			github="https://github.com/btylerh7/the-dad-a-base"
		>
			<p>
				This website was heavily inspired by the 1990s Geocities era of the internet. It
				features lots of custom SVGs that I made myself while learning how to make cool
				shapes and textures with SVGs by hand. The bricks, the wooden backgrounds, and even
				the clipboard icon were all made by me by hand!
			</p>
		</PortfolioCard>
		<PortfolioCard
			title="Bakery Shell"
			src={bakery}
			alt="A screenshot of a terminal window with the classic windows hill background, showing various terminal commands being run, such as 'ls' and 'cat'"
			tech="Rust, rustyline"
			github="https://github.com/btylerh7/bakery-shell"
			ps="P.S., I did most of this with the Rust standard library! The auto-complete required me to pull in a library, but I went as far as I could!"
		>
			<p>
				This project was made as a part of me using the{' '}
				<a href="https://codecrafters.io" target="_blank">
					Codecrafters
				</a>{' '}
				project "Build your own Shell". However, Codecrafters is not a traditional
				"tutorial" site, and this project is built by me. Codecrafters tells you what
				feature to add next, and you build that out however you see fit. You also choose
				your programming language, and I opted to go with Rust.
			</p>
			<p>
				Just a sample of some concepts I built: I built a custom parser, multiple shell
				builtins, auto-complete, and the ability to run external programs that are on the
				system path.
			</p>
		</PortfolioCard>
		<PortfolioCard
			title="Solitaire"
			src={solitaire}
			alt="A klondike solitaire board in a mid-game state"
			tech="Lua, Love2d, GLSL shaders"
			github="https://github.com/btylerh7/solitaire"
			ps="P.S. I added some cool functional programming concepts to lua tables such as list actions (filter, map, etc.)!"
		>
			<p>
				I have so many fond childhood memories of Solitaire. My grandfather had a computer
				with Windows 95 when I was growing up, and I remember sitting in his office playing
				Solitaire. I got it from my mom, because she would do the same thing when we went to
				their house.
			</p>
			<p>
				This was inspired by playing Balatro. Balatro is so fun, and I thought it was so
				cool to find out that it was written in Lua. I decided I wanted to take a shot at
				making a game with the framework!
			</p>
			<p>
				I had to do a lot of small tasks to really make this work. I had to calculate how to
				draw inidividual items on the screen, placing them in an object grid across the game
				window. I had to add logic for continuously checking for events, such as clicks and
				drags. I also created my own pixel art for the cards!
			</p>
			<p>
				Lastly, I had to set up the rules for solitaire games. There are three primary areas
				in Solitaire: the tableau, the foundation piles, and the stock. The tableau is the
				playing field. The cards are usually added to the foundation pile until no card
				remains anywhere else, usually split by suit and ordered from Ace to King. For
				example, in Klondike (the most well-known), there are seven rows in the tableau, one
				card in the first row, two in the second, and so on. Then there are four foundation
				files. I wrote rules out for Klondike and Free Cell.
			</p>
		</PortfolioCard>
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	main {
		color: white;
	}
</style>
